---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Observability Overview', href: '/docs/observability', active: true },
  { title: 'SIEM & Log Pipeline', href: '/docs/observability/siem' },
  { title: 'Kibana Dashboards', href: '/docs/observability/kibana' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'layers', text: 'Observability Layers', level: 2 },
  { id: 'sources', text: 'Telemetry Sources', level: 2 },
  { id: 'logging', text: 'Security Event Logging', level: 2 },
  { id: 'suricata', text: 'Suricata Log Ingestion', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Observability" description="How the honeypot system provides observability into adversary activities" section="Observability" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Observability has one job here: turn an ephemeral request decision into evidence that survives the
    session. Every scoring result, routing decision and network alert can leave the edge as structured data,
    and the SIEM side is designed so a missing Kibana never affects detection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layers">Observability Layers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Log Ingestion</strong>: Structured logs from the reverse proxy, Lua engines, and Suricata are centralized for SIEM consumption via Nginx error/security logs and Vector forwarding — over mutual TLS — to Elasticsearch/Kibana. See <a href="/docs/observability/siem#transport">Transport &amp; mTLS</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>SIEM Dashboards</strong>: High-level operational views of IDS alerts, threat overview, and web traffic.</li>
    <li><strong>Kibana Visualizations</strong>: Interactive dashboards and pattern report timelines.</li>
    <li><strong>Alerting & Reporting</strong>: AbuseIPDB reporting and reputation-based alerting.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The whole SIEM side is <strong>fail-open with respect to detection</strong>: it turns an ephemeral request
  decision into durable evidence, but nothing in the request path waits for it, so a SIEM outage never affects
  routing or scoring.</p>

  <h2 id="sources">Telemetry Sources</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Security events"
      href="#logging"
      icon="file"
      tag="Nginx error log"
      description="Structured JSON lines written by log_security_event(), read by Vector and forwarded to Elasticsearch."
      meta="SECURITY_EVENT prefix"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Suricata alerts"
      href="#suricata"
      icon="network"
      tag="eve.json"
      description="Parsed every 30 seconds by init_worker.lua for IP reputation and stored in the threat_intel shared dictionary."
      meta="30 s interval"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Routing traces"
      href="/docs/architecture#pipeline"
      icon="route"
      tag="Decision trace"
      description="Flat decision strings recorded per request so an analyst can reconstruct why a session was diverted."
      meta="stage-by-stage"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Dashboards"
      href="/docs/observability/kibana"
      icon="chart"
      tag="Kibana"
      description="Six saved dashboards plus a dedicated pattern-report timeline used for per-pattern visual evidence."
      meta="NDJSON managed"
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="logging">Security Event Logging</h2>
  <p><code>_G.utils.log_security_event(event_type, details)</code> appends one structured JSON line per event to
  the Nginx error log, prefixed with <code>SECURITY_EVENT</code>. Vector reads these lines and forwards them to
  Elasticsearch; the rules they must follow (JSON lines, empty tables encoded as <code>[]</code>, retries never
  blocking the edge) are the <a href="/docs/observability/siem#contracts">Pipeline Contracts</a>. The shared
  dictionaries that cache state between requests are listed in
  <a href="/docs/architecture/lua-internals#globals">Lua Internals</a>.</p>
  <p>The logger (<code>init.lua</code>) and a typical call from <code>router.lua</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>local log_entry = &#123;
    timestamp = ngx.time(),
    event_type = event_type,
    details = details,
    server_time = os.date("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
&#125;
ngx.log(ngx.WARN, "SECURITY_EVENT: ", cjson.encode(log_entry))</code></pre>
  <pre><code>_G.utils.log_security_event("routing_to_honeypot", &#123;
    reason = "high_risk_score",
    score = pattern_result.score,
    ip = remote_ip,
    pool = routing_decision.session_data.honeypot_pool,
    session_id = session_data.id
&#125;)</code></pre>
  <p>To watch them live: <code>docker compose logs -f reverse_proxy | grep SECURITY_EVENT</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="suricata">Suricata Log Ingestion</h2>
  <p><code>init_worker.lua</code> reads new <code>eve.json</code> alerts every 30 seconds, drops private and
  loopback sources, and stores a severity-graded score per IP in <code>threat_ips</code>, decayed when read. The
  scoring functions are on <a href="/docs/detection/suricata#integration">Suricata Network IDS</a>, and the reader's
  resume and size limits on <a href="/docs/architecture/lua-internals#suricata">Lua Internals</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Multi-layer observability, structured security events, empty-table fix, Suricata integration, offline dashboard generation.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> Dashboard drift, timeline capture fragility, no automated health monitoring, Vector complexity, same-host testing gap.</p>
</DocsLayout>
